abstract:
Let $ho$ be compactly supported on $D \subset \mathbb R^2$. Endow $\mathbb R^2$ with the metric $e^{ho}(dx_1^2 + dx_2^2)$. As $\delta 	o 0$ the set of Brownian loops centered in $D$ with length at least $\delta$ has measure $$ rac{	ext{area}(D)}{2\pi \delta} +  rac{1}{48\pi}(ho,ho)_{
abla}+ o(1).$$ When $ho$ is smooth, this follows from the classical Polyakov-Alvarez formula. We show that the above also holds if $ho$ is not smooth, e.g. if $ho$ is only Lipschitz. This fact can alternatively be expressed in terms of heat kernel traces, eigenvalue asymptotics, or zeta regularized determinants. Variants of this statement apply to more general non-smooth manifolds on which one considers all loops (not only those centered in a domain $D$). We also show that the $o(1)$ error is uniform for any family of $ho$ satisfying certain conditions. This implies that if we weight a measure $
u$ on this family by the ($\delta$-truncated) Brownian loop soup partition function, and take the vague $\delta 	o 0$ limit, we obtain a measure whose Radon-Nikodym derivative with respect to $
u$ is $\exp igl(  rac{1}{48\pi}(ho,ho)_{
abla} igr)$. When the measure is a certain regularized Liouville quantum gravity measure, a companion work [APPS20] shows that this weighting has the effect of changing the so-called central charge of the surface.
